S[5]=dict(lbl="Did attitudes have to change first?|What did change in people's thoughts?|Was the comparison really received?|Could unit size explain the result?|Why did the effect last?|What are the limits?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("What changed in people's thoughts, was the comparison really received, could something else explain the result, and why did comparative feedback work?",
 "Behavior changed a lot. This step checks the minds behind it and the alternative explanations. An <b>attitude</b> is how positive or negative you feel about a behavior; a <b>social norm</b> is what you think important others expect.",
 ["What happened to attitudes and beliefs","Whether the manipulation worked","The alternative explanation","Why the effect lasted, and the limits"]),
CARD("No: behavior changed a lot while attitudes hardly changed, so attitude change is not a prerequisite when tools aim at behavior itself.",
 "Many theories say attitudes change first, then intentions, then behavior (Fishbein &amp; Ajzen, 1975; Ajzen, 1991). Here feedback and comparison targeted behavior directly. The authors note attitudes were already quite positive at the start. <i>Our example:</i> taking the stairs because of an office stairs league, not because you changed your opinion about lifts.",
 "an exam option saying comparative feedback worked by changing attitudes first is wrong.",
 "what did change."),
CARD("After the campaign the comparison unit believed more strongly than the basic unit that switching off machines and lights saves energy, and its reported behavior rose; identification dropped in the basic unit.",
 "Table 1: attitudes and social norms did not change differently. After the campaign the comparison unit believed more strongly that switching off machines and lights saves energy (machines 4.6 v 4.0; lights 4.6 v 4.1, both significant after the campaign; for lights the change pattern itself was only borderline). Reported behavior rose there (3.1 → 3.7), not in the basic unit. Identification changed differently, but the significant part was a <b>drop</b> in the basic unit (4.2 → 3.6); the comparison unit went 3.8 → 4.0.",
 "heads changed a little, behavior a lot.",
 "was the comparison noticed?"),
CARD("Yes: the comparison unit was more curious about, more aware of and more competitive about other units' results.",
 "A <b>manipulation</b> is the condition the researchers create. Implementation checks: curious 3.9 v 3.3; aware 3.6 v 2.3; competitive 4.4 v 3.8.",
 "the comparison reached people as intended.",
 "the critic's alternative."),
CARD("Unlikely, the authors argue: communication and commitment did not differ, the comparison unit felt less supervision, starting attitudes were equal, and its lower start made saving harder.",
 "The critic's idea: a small unit talks more, can be supervised more closely and feels more committed. But communication and commitment did not differ, and the small unit felt <b>less</b> supervision (2.1 v 3.0), the opposite of what size predicts. One more difference: the comparison unit knew the goal better and found it more attainable (4.3 v 3.4; 4.0 v 3.2). The authors think the comparison caused this, through extra attention to the goal, and read it as felt competence; but it is a second way the units differed, and could be part of why the comparison unit did better.",
 "the result most likely comes from comparison, with one open alternative: goal knowledge.",
 "why it lasted."),
CARD("Because comparison may have strengthened identification with the group, making people intrinsically motivated and helping new habits form.",
 "<b>Intrinsically motivated</b> means doing something because it is valued in itself. If people only wanted to win, the effect should have stopped with the feedback; it lasted half a year. The transfer to behaviors without exact feedback fits this. These are the authors' interpretations; the questionnaire data support them only partly: identification rose only slightly in the comparison unit (3.8 to 4.0); the significant change was the basic unit's drop.",
 "lasting change likely came through group identity and habit, not only competition.",
 "the limits."),
CARD("The study could not test demoralization, and the tool needs specified, measured behavior with feedback that employees see as reliable and contingent on their own behavior.",
 "<b>Contingent</b> means clearly caused by one's own behavior. The comparison unit never had a long losing streak, so the study does not show that comparison cannot demoralize.",
 "comparative feedback is powerful, but only with reliable, behavior-specific feedback, and the losing-team risk is untested.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Behavior changed without attitude change; the comparison was received; size is an unlikely explanation; identity and habits may explain the lasting effect; demoralization remains untested.",
 [("Attitudes first?","No: not a prerequisite."),("What changed?","Beliefs and reported behavior; identification dropped in the basic unit."),("Received?","More curious, aware, competitive."),("Unit size?","Argued away; goal knowledge remains a difference."),("Why lasting?","Identification, intrinsic motivation, habits (interpretation)."),("Limits?","Demoralization untested; contingent feedback needed.")],
 ["Which statement is wrong? \"It worked by changing attitudes first.\"","Which alternative explanation remains? (better goal knowledge in the comparison unit)"])],
 guides=G("Six questions about Table 1, the checks and a critic appear one at a time.","<b>Interpret like the authors</b> and choose.","Read the numbers and the explanation after each answer."))

def run(path):
    s=open(path,encoding='utf-8').read()
    for n,d in S.items(): s=apply_step(s,W,n,d['lbl'],d['cards'],guides=d.get('guides'))
    open(path,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s);print('ok',list(S))
if __name__=='__main__': run(sys.argv[1])
